.\" proxmodctl(8) — hand-written roff, deliberately.
.\"
.\" Generating this from docs/cli.md would mean a build-dependency on pandoc or
.\" ronn for a file that changes when the CLI does, which is rarely. The cost of
.\" the tool is paid on every build by everyone; the cost of the duplication is
.\" paid by whoever changes proxmodctl's usage text. t/11 keeps the two in step.
.TH PROXMODCTL 8 "2026-08-27" "proxmod 0.2.2" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
proxmodctl \- administer the proxmod extension framework for Proxmox VE
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B proxmodctl
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B proxmodctl
is the administrator's entry point to proxmod, which loads extensions into the running
.B pvedaemon
and
.B pveproxy
without modifying any Proxmox-owned file.
.PP
Start with
.BR "proxmodctl status" .
Everything else in this page exists for when that answers badly.
.PP
.BR status ", " list ", " logs " and " doctor
change nothing and need no privilege beyond reading the journal.
.BR enable ", " disable ", " reapply
and every
.B patch
subcommand other than
.B status
require root.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Is proxmod working? Runs
.BR proxmod-verify (8)
and reports what it found.
.TP
.B "status --json"
The same report, machine-readable, for monitoring.
.TP
.B list
The extensions this host has installed, and the state of each: whether it is
loaded, disabled in its own manifest, masked by an override under
.IR /etc/proxmod/extensions.d ,
or dropped because something it requires is missing. Read off the disk rather
than out of a running daemon, so it still answers when the daemons are down.
.TP
.B reapply
Converge the systemd drop-ins onto what the package intends, and restart the
wrapped daemons only if something changed. Safe to run at any time; costs
nothing when there is nothing to do.
.TP
.B "reapply --force"
Restart the wrapped daemons even when nothing changed.
.TP
.B enable
Remove the kill switch and restart the daemons.
.TP
.B disable
Stop loading proxmod and restart the daemons exactly as Proxmox ships them. See
.B THE KILL SWITCH
below.
.TP
.BR logs " [" \-f ]
What proxmod said in the daemons' journals, without the rest of the journal.
.B \-f
follows.
.TP
.BI patch " subcommand"
The managed patch facility: \fBstatus\fR, \fBconverge\fR, \fBapply\fR \fIid\fR,
\fBrevert\fR \fIid\fR, \fBrevert-all\fR. With no subcommand, \fBstatus\fR.
Patching a Proxmox file is proxmod's last resort and is off by default on every
host; read
.I /usr/share/doc/proxmod/patching.md
before enabling a spec.
.TP
.B doctor
.B status
plus versions, the live
.B ExecStart
and
.B ExecReload
of both units, the registry and the patch state. This is the output to paste
into a bug report.
.SH THE KILL SWITCH
.B "proxmodctl disable"
creates
.IR /etc/proxmod/disabled .
The
.B ExecStart
wrapper checks for that file before it loads any proxmod code at all, so the
switch still works when proxmod itself is broken \- including when
.B proxmodctl
cannot run. The equivalent by hand, on a host you cannot otherwise recover:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
touch /etc/proxmod/disabled
systemctl restart pvedaemon pveproxy
.EE
.RE
.SH DAEMON RESTARTS
.BR enable ", " disable " and " reapply
restart
.B pvedaemon
and
.BR pveproxy .
.B Running guests are not affected.
Open web-interface sessions reconnect on their own. A daemon that does not come
back is the one outcome proxmod treats as unacceptable: its drop-ins are removed
and the daemon is restarted stock, leaving a host running unmodified Proxmox and
an explanation in the journal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR status ", " proxmod is healthy.
.TP
.B 1
A check failed, or the command did not do what it was asked to.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/proxmod/proxmod.conf
Global configuration. A conffile: your edits survive upgrades.
.TP
.I /etc/proxmod/extensions.d/
Administrator overrides, one drop-in manifest per extension. A file here
shadows the package-owned manifest of the same name; an empty file or a symlink
to
.I /dev/null
masks the extension entirely.
.TP
.I /usr/share/proxmod/extensions.d/
Package-owned manifests. Extension packages install here. Not for editing.
.TP
.I /etc/proxmod/disabled
The kill switch. Presence, not content, is what matters.
.TP
.I /var/lib/proxmod/
Convergence state and managed-patch records, including the backups
.B "patch revert"
restores from.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proxmod-verify (8),
.BR pvedaemon (8),
.BR pveproxy (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
.PP
.IR /usr/share/doc/proxmod/ ,
in particular
.IR cli.md ,
.I troubleshooting.md
and
.IR extension-manifest.md .
.SH BUGS
https://github.com/CrunchyMonkies/proxmod/issues
